        (11, 11, """`dc` is the slow baseline of the infrared signal (the "direct current" part, the steady light level). On a wrist it sits around 130,000 to 170,000 counts."""),
        (12, 12, """`hp1` and `hp2` are the memory of two further high-pass stages. They exist to remove the slow wobble caused by pressure on the wrist and by breathing."""),
        (13, 13, """`ac` is the filtered pulse waveform (the "alternating current" part): the heartbeat ripple with the baseline and the slow wobble removed."""),
        (14, 14, """`energy` is a running average of `ac` squared; its square root is the typical size of the pulse."""),
        (15, 15, """The history: a ring buffer holding the last 400 values of `ac`. A ring buffer is an array that is written round and round; when the end is reached the next write goes back to the start, overwriting the oldest value."""),
        (16, 16, """`head` is where the next value will be written; `filled` is how many entries hold real data (it stops at 400)."""),
        (17, 17, """Counts samples since the last scan; one scan is done every 100 samples (once a second)."""),
        (18, 18, """The beat period found by the previous scan, to compare against."""),
        (19, 19, """How many scans in a row have agreed on the period."""),
        (20, 20, """How many scans have passed since a heart rate was last accepted. It drives both the 3-scan hold and the 15-scan memory for the jump guard."""),
        (22, 22, """A private helper: how well the waveform matches itself shifted by a given number of samples."""),
        (24, 28, """The public results. `bpm` is the heart rate in beats per minute and is only meaningful when `valid` is true; `contact` says the sensor is against skin; `amplitude` is the pulse size in ADC counts."""),
        (30, 30, "Constructor."),
        (32, 33, """`reset()` throws away all history and state. `update()` takes one new infrared reading; the sketch calls it for every sample, 100 times a second."""),
